# João's sections. Edited directly since 31/10 (the old Joao_sections_DSS_report.docx and converter_joao.py are no longer used).
$figDirJ = Join-Path $PSScriptRoot 'figures'

$content['Data Sources'] = {
    Body 'The empirical reference for the model is the share of Banco Comercial Português (BCP), one of the most traded constituents of the PSI index. Daily adjusted closing prices and daily volumes (ticker BCP.LS) were downloaded from Yahoo Finance for January 2010 to October 2026 (4304 trading days), using the Python script download_daily.py. One-minute prices are also collected twice a week (archive_intraday.py), since Yahoo Finance only keeps the most recent days of intraday data.'
    Body 'The calibration targets were computed for 2023–2024 (510 trading days), a recent period without bank-specific crises. Two stress periods were used only as a reference for the plausibility of the shock scenarios: the resolution of Banco Espírito Santo (15 July to 30 September 2014) and the start of the COVID-19 pandemic (15 February to 30 April 2020).'
    Body 'The calibration period 2023–2024 was fixed in the project plan before any model run and was not changed afterwards. The year 2025 was not part of it; it also contains a market-wide sell-off in April 2025, when the BCP share fell 9.9% on 4 April and its daily volatility reached 3.7% in that month, against 1.1% to 2.7% in the other months of the year. A robustness check with 2025 and with each year separately is reported in Section 3.2.'
    Body 'The BES period starts on 15 July because the adjusted prices show artificial jumps of +12% and +24% at the end of June and the beginning of July 2014, caused by the BCP capital increase. All returns are logarithmic.'
}
$content['Stylised Facts of the BCP Stock'] = {
    Body 'Following the feedback on the project proposal, the stylised facts and their acceptance criteria were fixed before the calibration of the model, so that the targets could not be adjusted to the results. {T:facts} lists the seven facts, the value measured for BCP and the criterion used to decide whether the model reproduces each one. The same statistics are computed for the model by the script compare_model.py, using the last price of each 510-minute block as the daily close.'
    Tbl 'Stylised facts of the BCP stock (2023–2024) and calibration criteria' @('#', 'Stylised fact', 'Metric', 'BCP', 'Model matches if') @(
        @('1', 'Daily volatility', 'Standard deviation of daily returns', '0.0207', 'within ±25%'),
        @('2', 'Heavy tails', 'Excess kurtosis', '2.41', '> 1 and within ±50%'),
        @('3', 'Volatility clustering', 'Lag-1 autocorrelation of absolute returns', '0.080', '> 0.05'),
        @('4', 'Absence of return autocorrelation', 'Lag-1 autocorrelation of returns', '0.004', 'between −0.1 and 0.1'),
        @('5', 'Frequency of large moves', 'Share of days with |r| > 3%', '12.9%', 'within ±50%'),
        @('6', 'Volume–volatility relation', 'Correlation between volume and |r|', '0.636', '> 0'),
        @('7', 'Volume persistence', 'Lag-1 autocorrelation of daily volume', '0.470', '> 0')
    ) @(0.8, 3.6, 5.2, 1.8, 4.6) 'facts'
    Body 'The BCP share shows the typical behaviour of financial returns: returns are not autocorrelated, the distribution has heavy tails, large moves tend to be followed by large moves, and trading volume is higher on volatile days and persistent from one day to the next. The last fact was added on 31 October 2026, before any tuning of the model, so that the calibration covers both price and volume, as recommended in the feedback on the proposal. Volatility clustering is weak (0.080) and close to the acceptance threshold, so it is expected to be the hardest fact to reproduce.'
    Body 'As a robustness check, {T:robust} computes the same statistics for each year separately and for 2025. Most values are of the same order of magnitude as the targets: daily volatility between 1.9% and 2.2%, excess kurtosis between 2.0 and 3.0, 11% to 15% of days with moves above 3% and a volume-volatility correlation between 0.57 and 0.70. The facts that vary most from year to year are volatility clustering (0.06 to 0.18), the autocorrelation of returns (-0.09 to 0.07) and volume persistence (0.16 to 0.49), which are also the facts that were hardest to reproduce in the calibration (Section 6.3). The April 2025 sell-off is visible mainly in volatility clustering (0.177, more than twice the target), while the mean daily volatility of 2025 (1.93%) is close to that of the calibration period. The targets were not changed after this check.'
    Tbl 'Stylised facts of the BCP stock in other periods (robustness check)' @('Statistic', '2023-24 (target)', '2023', '2024', '2025') @(
        @('Daily volatility', '0.0207', '0.0217', '0.0197', '0.0193'),
        @('Excess kurtosis', '2.41', '2.01', '2.96', '2.94'),
        @('Autocorrelation of |r|', '0.080', '0.063', '0.096', '0.177'),
        @('Autocorrelation of r', '0.004', '0.072', '-0.078', '-0.086'),
        @('Days with |r| > 3%', '12.9%', '14.6%', '11.0%', '12.6%'),
        @('Correlation volume-|r|', '0.636', '0.609', '0.701', '0.569'),
        @('Autocorrelation of volume', '0.470', '0.495', '0.333', '0.164')
    ) @(5.2, 2.7, 2.7, 2.7, 2.7) 'robust'
    Body '{T:stress} compares the calm period with the two stress periods. During a crisis the daily volatility roughly doubles and the share of days with moves above 3%, the range in which the collars act, roughly triples. The worst days reach −16%, so the stylised shock of the scenarios (−10%, Chapter 7) is of a plausible order of magnitude.'
    Tbl 'BCP statistics in the calm period and in two stress periods' @('Statistic', 'Calm 2023–24', 'BES 2014', 'COVID-19 2020') @(
        @('Trading days', '510', '55', '51'),
        @('Daily volatility', '2.1%', '4.0%', '4.4%'),
        @('Days with |r| > 3%', '13%', '36%', '41%'),
        @('Worst day', '−9.5%', '−16.3%', '−16.5%'),
        @('Best day', '+9.3%', '+8.4%', '+6.7%'),
        @('Maximum drawdown', '23%', '32%', '54%')
    ) @(4.6, 3.8, 3.8, 3.8) 'stress'
    Body 'The model is not meant to reproduce these historical crashes: the shocks are stylised and are used to compare interruption rules. Since each stress period has only about 50 trading days, higher-order statistics such as kurtosis and autocorrelations are unreliable there and are not used as targets.'
}
$content['Real Information Shocks'] = {
    Body 'In the first versions of the model the fundamental value changed through stylised news events drawn at random (about one per day, with a size of ±2%). Although the kurtosis and volatility of BCP imply a similar order of magnitude under a mixture-of-distributions model (Clark, 1973), these events were not observed but chosen, and they merged every source of information into a single random process. Following the feedback on the realism of the model, the random news were replaced by the information shocks that actually hit BCP in 2023–2024, measured with standard event-study methods (MacKinlay, 1997). The model now replays these shocks day by day over the 509 trading days of the calibration period, so that its output can be compared with BCP on the same days and with the same events.'
    Body 'Four sources were used, all public. First, the 84 official announcements of BCP to the market in 2023–2024 (results, shareholder transactions, provisions for the Polish mortgage portfolio, ratings, capital and debt issues, governance), collected from the investor relations pages of the bank. Second, the monetary policy decisions of the European Central Bank. Third, the main Portuguese political events of the period (the resignation of the Prime Minister on 7 November 2023, the dissolution of Parliament, the March 2024 election, the new government and the State Budget votes). Fourth, every change in the sovereign rating or outlook of the Portuguese Republic by the four agencies. The political and rating events were selected by category before looking at the price of BCP, so that the list is not chosen by its effect. The 121 events and their sources are listed in data/raw/noticias_bcp.csv.'
    Body 'The common factor is the European banking sector, measured by the EURO STOXX Banks index (through the exchange-traded fund EXX1.DE). A regression of the daily BCP return on the sector return, estimated on days without any BCP announcement or Portuguese event, gives a beta of 0.98, so that the sector shock of each day is 0.98 times the real sector return. Events that moved all European banks, such as the failures of Silicon Valley Bank and Credit Suisse in March 2023 or the Italian windfall tax on banks in August 2023, enter the model through this channel with their real size and timing. The shock of each announcement is the abnormal return of BCP on its reaction day, that is, the part of the BCP return not explained by the sector. The reaction day was measured for each type of announcement: results, shareholder transactions, the Polish provisions, governance and rating changes move the price on the following day, because they are published after the close, while debt issues, results of subsidiaries and political events move it on the same day.'
    Tbl 'Sources of the daily variance of the BCP return, 2023–2024' @('Source', 'Measure', 'Share of variance') @(
        @('European banking sector', 'Beta 0.98 to the EURO STOXX Banks; real daily returns', '37.6%'),
        @('BCP announcements and Portuguese events', 'Abnormal return on 91 reaction days', '18.8%'),
        @('Portuguese market (other PSI stocks)', 'Beta 0.36 to the part not explained by the sector (t = 2.7)', '1.1%'),
        @('Not identified', 'Residual on days without events; enters as block-bootstrapped draws', '42.5%')
    ) @(5.0, 7.5, 3.0) 'shockSources'
    Body 'The choice of the external factors was also tested with data. Each candidate factor was added to the regression of the BCP return on the sector, on days without events, and kept only if it explained a significant additional part of the variance ({T:factorTests}). The general European market, the US market, US volatility, the euro-dollar rate, oil and the euro-zloty rate add nothing once the banking sector is included: events of this kind reach BCP through the banking sector. The PSI index seemed to add 9%, but BCP is itself a large constituent of the index; the average of the other PSI stocks adds only 1.1%, a small but significant Portuguese factor that was included with the rule, fixed before the test, that a factor enters if |t| > 2. ECB decision days show no additional variance of BCP.'
    Tbl 'External factors tested for the BCP return, 2023–2024 (days without events)' @('Candidate factor', 'R2 alone', 'Additional R2 after the sector', 't', 'Decision') @(
        @('European banking sector (EURO STOXX Banks)', '0.429', '—', '—', 'Included'),
        @('Portuguese market without BCP (14 PSI stocks)', '0.170', '0.011', '2.6', 'Included'),
        @('PSI index (includes BCP)', '0.399', '0.093', '8.2', 'Not used: contains BCP'),
        @('European market (EURO STOXX 50)', '0.227', '0.000', '-0.3', 'Excluded'),
        @('US market (S&P 500, previous day)', '0.002', '0.001', '-0.7', 'Excluded'),
        @('US volatility (VIX, previous day)', '0.003', '0.000', '0.3', 'Excluded'),
        @('Euro-dollar', '0.000', '0.000', '0.5', 'Excluded'),
        @('Brent oil', '0.020', '0.000', '-0.1', 'Excluded'),
        @('Euro-zloty (Polish subsidiary)', '0.003', '0.000', '-0.5', 'Excluded')
    ) @(6.0, 1.8, 2.8, 1.2, 3.6) 'factorTests'
    Body 'About 42% of the daily variance of BCP cannot be associated with any identified source of information. This part is closely linked to trading activity: its correlation with abnormal volume is 0.43, and its variance on the quarter of days with the highest volume is 2.25 times its average. This is consistent with the evidence that a large part of stock price movements is not explained by public news (Roll, 1988; Cutler, Poterba & Summers, 1989) and that trading itself generates volatility (French & Roll, 1986). The model was first run without this part, to test whether the trading of the agents could generate it (round 9, Section 6.3). The ANOVA and the screening experiment showed that no combination of the unobservable parameters does so without creating price reversals that BCP does not show: in BCP these movements have no memory from one day to the next (autocorrelation 0.007), like new information. They were therefore added as an unidentified information channel: on each day without an identified event, the model draws one of the 417 residual movements of BCP on such days in 2023–2024. The draws are made in blocks of 8 consecutive days starting at a random day (a block bootstrap, with block length close to the cube root of the sample size), which keeps the short memory of these movements (autocorrelation of their absolute value 0.078) without replaying them in their real order, and a different sequence is drawn in each replication. The model therefore uses the size, shape and short memory of these movements, but not their dates.'
    Body 'Using only the unidentified movements of 2023–2024 assumes that their size is typical of the present BCP. This was checked year by year from 2010 to 2026, with the betas of the model, using a robust measure of dispersion (1.4826 times the median absolute deviation) that is not distorted by the price jumps caused by the capital increases of the bank ({T:residYears}). During the crisis of the bank (2011–2017: sovereign debt crisis, state support and several capital increases) the unidentified movements were about twice as large as today. From 2018 onwards their size is stable, between 0.8% and 1.7% per day, with a median of 1.28%; the 1.24% of 2023–2024 is typical of this period, so the pool was not extended. The same table explains part of the out-of-sample results (Section 6.4): 2025 and 2026 had smaller unidentified movements than usual (1.10% and 0.81%). In a banking crisis like that of 2011–2017 this information would be about twice as large, so the crash scenarios of the model, which represent the present BCP, are conservative in this respect.'
    Tbl 'Size of the unidentified daily movements of BCP by year (robust standard deviation, betas of 2023–2024)' @('Period', 'Years', 'Robust s.d. per day', 'Context') @(
        @('Before the crisis', '2010', '1.25%', '—'),
        @('Crisis of the bank', '2011–2017', '1.9% to 2.6%', 'Sovereign debt crisis, state support, capital increases'),
        @('Present BCP', '2018–2022', '1.13% to 1.69%', 'Includes COVID-19 (2020: 1.41%) and 2022 (1.69%)'),
        @('Calibration pool', '2023–2024', '1.31% / 1.16% (1.24% together)', 'Median of 2018–2026: 1.28%'),
        @('Out-of-sample test', '2025–2026', '1.10% / 0.81%', 'Calmer than usual')
    ) @(3.4, 2.4, 4.0, 6.0) 'residYears'
    Body 'The same data were used to measure the daily dynamics of trading volume, replacing values that had been calibrated before. A regression of the logarithm of daily volume (in deviation from its 60-day moving average) on its previous value, on announcement days and on the absolute sector and unidentified movements of the same day gives a persistence of 0.41 (t = 11.3), an increase of 0.33 on announcement days (t = 7.2), an increase of 0.14 for each 1.65% of sector move (t = 4.7) and of 0.285 for each 1.65% of unidentified move (t = 9.8); these values are used for the activity level of the noise traders. The same data show no increase in volume on the day after a large price move (coefficient -0.065, t = -2.6), which contradicts the attention mechanism introduced in round 8b of the calibration; that mechanism was therefore switched off. Finally, the initial price of the model is the closing price of BCP on 30 December 2022 (0.1464 €), and the sector shock of each day is spread evenly over the session, since one-minute BCP data show the variance spread almost uniformly across the continuous session.'
}

$content['Key Assumptions'] = {
    Body 'Public data on the order book and on investor behaviour in Euronext Lisbon is scarce. For this reason, every value that does not come directly from data is recorded in an assumptions register (Appendix E), with its justification and the way it is tested: calibrated against the stylised facts (C), included in the sensitivity analysis (S), covered by the extreme condition tests (V), or fixed as a rule or simplification (—). The most relevant assumptions are:'
    Bullets @('a single stock (BCP) is modelled, without contagion to other stocks;', 'the trading day is a continuous 510-minute session, without opening and closing auctions;', 'all orders are limit orders, and an aggressive limit order plays the role of a market order;', 'the number of agents of each type and their behavioural parameters are not observable; they are taken from the agent-based market literature and calibrated against the stylised facts;', 'information enters the model as the real shocks of 2023–2024 (European banking sector, Portuguese market, BCP announcements and Portuguese political and rating events; Section 3.3); the remaining 42% of the daily variance, not associated with any identified source, enters as random draws (in blocks) of the real unidentified movements of 2023–2024;', 'the crash scenarios add a stylised shock inspired by the BES resolution, not a reproduction of a historical crash.')
    Body 'Following the feedback received during the project, the register was reviewed to identify assumptions that were unrealistic, critical for the research question or not supported by data, and the model was changed accordingly. First, fundamentalists and trend followers no longer stop trading during a reservation period: the Euronext Trading Manual allows members to enter, modify and cancel orders while a stock is reserved, so their orders wait in the pending queue and take part in the reopening auction (parameter tradeDuringHalt). Second, the random news events were replaced by the real information shocks of 2023–2024 (Section 3.3), and the choice of the external factors was tested with data. Third, the dynamics of trading volume and the initial price are measured from BCP data instead of calibrated. Fourth, the reopening auction follows the Euronext rules for PSI constituents (a ±6% auction collar and a new reservation at the collar limit when the auction cannot trade within it). An attention mechanism for trend followers (Barber & Odean, 2008), added in round 8b, was removed because BCP volume does not rise after large price moves. What remains without data is stated as such: the number and weight of each type of investor and their behavioural parameters are calibrated and tested in the sensitivity analysis, and two critical assumptions remain (investors have no budget constraint and the market maker has no inventory limit and never withdraws its quotes), which make the simulated market more resilient than the real one during a crash and are discussed as limitations in Chapter 10.'
}
$content['Fundamentalist agent type'] = {
    Body 'Fundamentalists represent informed investors who trade on the gap between the market price and their own estimate of the value of the share. They buy when the share is cheap and sell when it is expensive, which pulls the price back towards its fundamental value and stabilises the market.'
    Body 'Each fundamentalist has its own estimate of the value, value() = fundamentalValue × (1 + bias), where bias is drawn once per agent from a normal distribution with mean 0 and standard deviation 2%. This heterogeneity of beliefs avoids all fundamentalists trading at the same time and in the same direction. In the calibrated model the fundamentalists have no budget constraint (10^9 € in cash and 10^9 shares, as the noise traders), because limited endowments made the market fade during the year (Section 6.3).'
    Tbl 'Key parameters, variables and functions of the Fundamentalist agent type' @('Name', 'Type', 'Value', 'Description') @(
        @('nFundamentalists', 'Parameter (Main, int)', '40', 'Number of fundamentalists'),
        @('fundRate', 'Parameter (Main)', '0.2', 'Decisions per minute of each fundamentalist (one every 5 min on average)'),
        @('fundBand', 'Parameter (Main)', '0.01', 'Minimum gap between price and value that triggers a trade'),
        @('cash', 'Variable (double)', '10^9', 'Available cash (EUR); 5000 before calibration'),
        @('position', 'Variable (int)', '10^9', 'Number of shares held; 10 000 before calibration'),
        @('bias', 'Variable (double)', 'Normal(0, 2%)', 'Individual valuation error'),
        @('value()', 'Function (double)', '—', 'Value of the share estimated by the agent')
    ) @(3.2, 3.6, 2.6, 6.6) 'fundParams'
    Body 'The behaviour is defined by the statechart in {F:fundChart} and {T:fundStates}. The composite state Active contains the trading logic. At random instants, with rate fundRate per minute, the agent leaves Watching and compares the mid price with its estimate. It buys if the price is more than fundBand below value() and it can afford at least 100 shares, and sells if the price is more than fundBand above value() and it holds at least 100 shares.'
    Fig (Join-Path $figDirJ 'fundamentalist_statechart.png') 'Statechart of the Fundamentalist agent type' 12.0 'fundChart'
    Tbl 'States and transitions of the Fundamentalist agent type statechart' @('State', 'Meaning', 'Transitions') @(
        @('Watching', 'Waiting for the next decision (initial state of Active)', 'To the branch decide at rate fundRate'),
        @('decide (branch)', 'Compares the mid price with value()', 'To Buying if cheap and cash allows; to Selling if expensive and shares allow; to OutOfCash if no cash and no shares; otherwise (default) back to Watching'),
        @('Buying', 'Submits a buy order', 'Back to Watching immediately (timeout 0)'),
        @('Selling', 'Submits a sell order', 'Back to Watching immediately (timeout 0)'),
        @('OutOfCash', 'No cash and fewer than 100 shares (never reached in the calibrated model)', 'Back to Watching after 60 min'),
        @('Halted', 'Trading suspended during a reservation (only when tradeDuringHalt is false)', 'From Active on message HALT if tradeDuringHalt is false; back to Active (Watching) on message REOPEN')
    ) @(3.0, 5.2, 7.8) 'fundStates'
    Body 'The size of each order grows with the mispricing: a buy order has 100 + 20 000 × (value/mid − 1) shares, limited by the available cash, and a sell order has 100 + 20 000 × (mid/value − 1) shares, limited by the shares held. The limit price never exceeds the agent''s own estimate and stays within 0.2% of the mid price, so fundamentalists rarely move the price by themselves. The cash and the position are updated by the function onFill of the Main agent every time one of their orders is executed.'
    Body 'During a reservation period the Stock agent broadcasts a HALT message, which is delivered directly to the statechart of every fundamentalist. In the first version of the model the transition to Halted, which leaves the border of the composite state Active, stopped the agent whatever its current state until the REOPEN message arrived after the reopening auction. In the calibrated model this transition has the guard !main.tradeDuringHalt and tradeDuringHalt is true, so fundamentalists keep deciding during a reservation; their orders wait in the pending queue of the Stock agent and take part in the reopening auction, as the Euronext Trading Manual allows (Section 3.4). Setting tradeDuringHalt to false restores the original behaviour.'
    Body 'Two tests confirmed this behaviour. With noise traders only, the price follows a random walk with no anchor; with 40 fundamentalists it oscillates within about ±2% of the fundamental value of 0.50 €. In a deliberately volatile market (noiseSigma = 0.05), every HALT was followed by a REOPEN 3.01 minutes later and, with tradeDuringHalt set to false, the fundamentalists stayed in Halted during each reservation.'
}
$content['APPENDIX E: Assumptions Register'] = {
    Body 'Test: C = calibrated against the stylised facts; S = sensitivity analysis; V = extreme condition tests; — = fixed rule or simplification. The Value column is updated with the calibrated values at the end of the project.'
    Tbl 'Assumptions register' @('#', 'Assumption', 'Value', 'Justification / source', 'Test') @(
        @('1', 'A single stock (BCP), without contagion', '—', 'Phase 1 scope, as recommended', '—'),
        @('2', 'Collar rules for PSI constituents: ±3% dynamic (last traded price), ±8% static (opening or previous reference price), 3-min reservation, 10 min for a consecutive static reservation in the same direction, reopening auction', 'real rules', 'Euronext Trading safeguards (consulted on 31/10/2026); appendix to Trading Manuals 4-01 (2025), group P0 Equities PSI', '—'),
        @('3', 'Continuous 510-min trading day (08:00-16:30 Lisbon time, i.e. 09:00-17:30 CET); no overnight, pre-opening, opening or closing auctions', '510 min', 'Hours confirmed in the Euronext appendix to Trading Manuals 4-01 (2025); the static reference changes every 510 min', '—'),
        @('4', 'Initial price and initial fundamental value', '0.1464 €', 'Closing price of BCP on 30 December 2022 (start of the calibration period)', '—'),
        @('5', 'Tick size', '0.0001 €', 'Simplification (Euronext uses a tick size table)', '—'),
        @('6', 'All orders are limit orders; an aggressive order acts as a market order', '—', 'Order book simplification', '—'),
        @('7', 'Mean order lifetime (orderLife), drawn as orderLife × U(0.3, 1.7)', '35 min', 'No data on cancellations in Lisbon', 'C, S'),
        @('8', 'Number of agents (nFundamentalists, nChartists, nNoise)', '40 / 13 / 30', 'Agent-based market literature (e.g. Lux & Marchesi, 1999); trend followers reduced from 30 to 13 in the calibration', 'C'),
        @('9', 'Fundamentalist band and decision rate (fundBand, fundRate)', '0.01 / 0.2 per min', 'Literature (e.g. Chiarella & Iori, 2002)', 'C, S'),
        @('10', 'Valuation error of each fundamentalist (bias)', 'Normal(0, 2%)', 'Heterogeneous beliefs', '—'),
        @('11', 'Trend follower decision rate and threshold (chartRate, trendThreshold)', '0.2 per min / 0.005', 'Literature (e.g. Lux & Marchesi, 1999)', 'C, S'),
        @('12', 'Memory of the moving average (maWindow)', '50 min', 'Intermediate intraday horizon', 'C, S'),
        @('13', 'Trend follower network; only trend follower neighbours receive the signal', 'small world, 6 links', 'As in the Market Model used in class', '—'),
        @('14', 'Herding threshold and panic duration (herdThreshold, panicDuration)', '2 / 10 min', 'Not observable', 'C, S'),
        @('15', 'Noise trader rate and price dispersion (noiseRate, noiseSigma)', '1 per min / 0.2%', 'Baseline volume and liquidity', 'C, S'),
        @('16', 'Market maker spread and quantity (mmSpread, mmQty)', '0.4% / 2000', 'No data; tested at extreme values', 'S, V'),
        @('17', 'Stylised news shock (shockSize, shockTime)', '−10%, day 60', 'Inspired by BES; not a reproduction', 'S'),
        @('18', 'Probability of panic of a trend follower after the shock (shockPanicProb)', '0.3', 'Not observable', 'S'),
        @('19', 'Information shocks: real 2023–2024 shocks of the European banking sector (beta 0.98), of the Portuguese market (beta 0.36) and of BCP announcements and Portuguese political and rating events (abnormal returns on the measured reaction day), replayed day by day; the random news of earlier versions are switched off', 'choques_reais.csv', 'Yahoo Finance, BCP investor relations, ECB, news sources (Section 3.3); external factors tested with data', '—'),
        @('20', 'No budget constraint for fundamentalists and trend followers (cash, position)', '10^9 € / 10^9 shares', 'Calibrated: limited endowments (5000 € / 10 000 shares) made the market fade during the year', 'C'),
        @('21', 'Fundamentalist orders: 100 + 20 000 × deviation from value; limit up to ±0.2% of the mid', '—', 'Larger orders for larger mispricing', 'C'),
        @('22', 'Fundamentalist without cash or shares waits before trying again', '60 min', 'Simplification; not reached in the calibrated model', '—'),
        @('23', 'Trend follower orders: up to 500 shares at ±0.3% of the mid; panic up to 2000 shares at −1%', '—', 'Panic sells large quantities below the market', 'C'),
        @('24', 'Trend followers forget sell signals', '30 min', 'Not observable', '—'),
        @('25', 'Noise traders have no cash or short-selling constraints', '—', 'Source of baseline liquidity', '—'),
        @('26', 'Noise trader order quantity: U(100, 1000) multiplied by the daily activity level', 'U(100, 1000) x activity', 'Calibrated (round 7): gives volume persistence and links volume to news', 'C'),
        @('27', 'Dynamic collar measured against the last traded price', 'lastPrice', 'Euronext rule: the dynamic reference price is the last traded price', 'V'),
        @('28', 'Instantaneous reopening auction: maximum volume, ties broken by proximity to the last price', '—', 'Simplified auction rule', 'V'),
        @('29', 'During a reservation all agents keep submitting orders, which wait for the reopening auction (relaxed in round 8b; until then fundamentalists and trend followers stopped)', 'tradeDuringHalt = true', 'Euronext Trading Manual: members can enter, modify and cancel orders during a reservation', 'C'),
        @('30', 'BES stress period starts on 15 July 2014', '15/07–30/09/2014', 'Excludes the price jumps caused by the BCP capital increase', '—'),
        @('31', 'Source of real data: Yahoo Finance adjusted daily prices (BCP.LS)', '2010–2026', 'Free source with a long history', '—'),
        @('32', 'Reservations last exactly the Euronext minimum; in the real market they can be extended', '3 / 10 min', 'Simplification', 'S'),
        @('33', 'The static reference price is the last price of the previous day and is reset to the reopening auction price after each reservation; Euronext defines it as the opening price of the day or the previous reference price', '-', 'Simplification (no opening auction in the model)', 'V'),
        @('34', 'Reopening auction collar (auctionCollar): the auction price stays within ±6% of the last price before the reservation', '±6%', 'Euronext rule for PSI constituents (appendix to Trading Manuals 4-01, column Col U); added on 04/11 after a replication fell from 0.84 € to 0.02 € through successive auctions', 'V'),
        @('35', 'If the reopening auction cannot trade within the ±6% collar, the last price moves to the collar limit and a new reservation follows', 'collar limit', 'Euronext Trading Manual (5 July 2021), section 4.2.4: the upper or lower threshold becomes the new reference price; added on 08/11 after a replication was stuck in 1485 reservations', 'V'),
        @('36', 'Daily activity level of noise traders: log(activity) has persistence activityPersist, a random part activitySigma, and rises by activityNewsJump on announcement days, by activitySectorJump and by activityResidJump for each 1.65% of sector and unidentified move', '0.41 / 0.05 / 0.33 / 0.14 / 0.285', 'Persistence and jumps estimated jointly by regression of BCP volume, 2023–2024 (Section 3.3); activitySigma calibrated', 'C'),
        @('37', 'Attention of trend followers (decision rate multiplied by an attention level that rises after large daily moves)', 'switched off', 'Added in round 8b (Barber & Odean, 2008); removed because BCP volume does not rise after large moves (t = -2.6)', '—'),
        @('38', 'Final calibrated model', 'Round 11 (7 of 7 facts)', 'Real information shocks, unidentified information in blocks, measured volume, no attention; agent parameters unchanged since round 9', 'C'),
        @('39', 'Timing of the shocks within the day: the sector and Portuguese market shocks are spread evenly over the 510 minutes; announcements enter at the opening of the reaction day', '—', 'One-minute BCP data show the variance spread almost uniformly over the session; the exact time of announcements is not available', '—'),
        @('40', 'Unidentified information (about 42% of the variance of BCP): on days without events, one of the 417 real residual movements of 2023–2024 is drawn, in blocks of 8 consecutive days starting at random; different in each replication', 'block of 8 days', 'ANOVA and screening: the agents cannot generate it without reversals; residuals have no memory (0.007); block length about the cube root of 417', 'C'),
        @('41', 'Influential parameters without data: number of trend followers and noise traders, trend follower threshold, order lifetime, order rate of noise traders, decision rates, panic duration', 'see Appendix E rows 7-15', 'Identified by the ANOVA and the screening experiment (Section 6.3); tested in the sensitivity analysis of the scenarios', 'S'),
        @('42', 'Out-of-sample test 2025-2026: betas, reaction days and unidentified movements taken from 2023-2024; only the real 2025-2026 returns of the sector and of the Portuguese market and the dated events are used', '460 days', 'Prevents the test data from being used to adjust the model (Section 6.4)', 'V')
        @('43', 'The unidentified movements of 2023–2024 are representative of the present BCP', 'robust s.d. 1.24% per day', 'Yearly measurement 2010–2026: median of 2018–2026 is 1.28%; in the crisis of 2011–2017 they were about twice as large (Section 3.3)', '—'),
        @('44', 'Acceptance of the stylised facts: initial criteria set by judgement, complemented by the 95% block-bootstrap interval of each fact of BCP', 'blocks of 10 days, 3000 samples', 'A model value inside the interval cannot be distinguished from BCP by chance (Section 6.3)', 'C')
        @('45', 'Out-of-sample test 2020–2021 (COVID-19, 509 days): same rules as the 2025–2026 test; the state of emergency is not added as an event because it is not in the categories fixed before the tests', 'initial price 0.2028 EUR', 'Tests the model in a crisis without changing any parameter (Section 6.4): 7/7 initial criteria, 5/7 data-based intervals', 'C')
    ) @(0.8, 6.0, 2.6, 5.2, 1.4) 'assumptions'
}

$referencesJoao = @('Chiarella, C., & Iori, G. (2002). A simulation analysis of the microstructure of double auction markets. Quantitative Finance, 2(5), 346–353.', 'Lux, T., & Marchesi, M. (1999). Scaling and criticality in a stochastic multi-agent model of a financial market. Nature, 397, 498–500.')

$content['Trend Follower agent type and Herding'] = {
    Body 'Trend followers (chartists) ignore the value of the share and trade on the recent direction of the price. They buy when the price is above its moving average and sell when it is below. When they sell, they warn their neighbours in a social network, and an agent that receives enough warnings panics and sells a large quantity below the market. Trend following and herding amplify price movements, which is what drives the price towards the collars.'
    Body 'Like the fundamentalists, trend followers have no budget constraint in the calibrated model (Section 6.3). Each trend follower compares the last traded price with the moving average ma of the Stock agent, which is updated every minute with a memory of maWindow minutes. {T:chartParams} lists the parameters, variables, functions and events of this agent type.'
    Tbl 'Key parameters, variables, functions and events of the Trend Follower agent type' @('Name', 'Type', 'Value', 'Description') @(
        @('nChartists', 'Parameter (Main, int)', '13', 'Number of trend followers (30 before calibration)'),
        @('chartRate', 'Parameter (Main)', '0.2', 'Decisions per minute of each trend follower (one every 5 min on average)'),
        @('trendThreshold', 'Parameter (Main)', '0.005', 'Minimum gap between the last price and the moving average that triggers a trade'),
        @('herdThreshold', 'Parameter (Main, int)', '2', 'Number of sell signals from neighbours that triggers panic'),
        @('panicDuration', 'Parameter (Main)', '10', 'Duration of a panic episode (min)'),
        @('shockPanicProb', 'Parameter (Main)', '0.3', 'Probability of panic when a negative news shock arrives'),
        @('cash', 'Variable (double)', '10^9', 'Available cash (EUR); 5000 before calibration'),
        @('position', 'Variable (int)', '10^9', 'Number of shares held; 10 000 before calibration'),
        @('sellSignals', 'Variable (int)', '0', 'Sell signals received from neighbours since the last reset'),
        @('signalNeighbours()', 'Function', '-', 'Delivers a SELL_SIGNAL message to the statechart of every trend follower neighbour'),
        @('forget', 'Event (every 30 min)', '-', 'Resets sellSignals, so that only recent warnings count'),
        @('attention', 'Variable (Main, double)', '1 (initial)', 'Attention level, updated daily from the absolute daily return'),
        @('attPersist', 'Parameter (Main)', '0.7', 'Persistence of the attention level from one day to the next'),
        @('attWeight', 'Parameter (Main)', '0.6', 'Sensitivity of attention to the daily price move'),
        @('useAttChart', 'Parameter (Main, boolean)', 'false', 'Multiplies chartRate by attention (switched off in the final model, Section 3.3)'),
        @('tradeDuringHalt', 'Parameter (Main, boolean)', 'true', 'Fundamentalists and trend followers keep trading during reservations')
    ) @(3.4, 3.6, 1.6, 7.4) 'chartParams'
    Body 'The trend followers are connected by a small-world network built in the start-up code of the Main agent, as in the Market Model used in class. The agents are placed on a ring and each one is linked to the next three, which gives about six neighbours per agent; with probability 5% a link goes to a random trend follower instead, creating shortcuts across the ring. The network only connects trend followers, so that the warnings are not lost on agents that do not react to them.'
    Body 'The behaviour is defined by the statechart in {F:chartChart} and {T:chartStates}. At random instants, with rate chartRate per minute, the agent leaves Neutral and compares the last price with the moving average. It buys up to 500 shares at 0.3% above the mid price if the price is more than trendThreshold above the average, and sells up to 500 shares at 0.3% below the mid price, warning its neighbours, if the price is more than trendThreshold below the average.'
    Fig (Join-Path $figDirJ 'chartist_statechart.png') 'Statechart of the Trend Follower agent type' 12.0 'chartChart'
    Tbl 'States and transitions of the Trend Follower agent type statechart' @('State', 'Meaning', 'Transitions') @(
        @('Neutral', 'Waiting for the next decision (initial state of Active)', 'To the branch decide at rate chartRate; to the branch herd on message SELL_SIGNAL (sellSignals + 1); to Panic on message SHOCK with probability shockPanicProb'),
        @('decide (branch)', 'Compares the last price with the moving average', 'To TrendBuy if the price is above the average and cash allows; to TrendSell if it is below and shares allow; otherwise (default) back to Neutral'),
        @('TrendBuy', 'Submits a buy order', 'Back to Neutral immediately (timeout 0)'),
        @('TrendSell', 'Submits a sell order and warns the neighbours', 'Back to Neutral immediately (timeout 0)'),
        @('herd (branch)', 'Counts the warnings received', 'To Panic if sellSignals reaches herdThreshold and the agent holds shares; otherwise (default) back to Neutral'),
        @('Panic', 'Sells up to 2000 shares at 1% below the mid price and warns the neighbours', 'Back to Neutral after panicDuration minutes, resetting sellSignals'),
        @('Halted', 'Trading suspended during a reservation (only when tradeDuringHalt is false)', 'From Active on message HALT if tradeDuringHalt is false; back to Active (Neutral) on message REOPEN')
    ) @(3.0, 5.0, 8.0) 'chartStates'
    Body 'Herding spreads as a cascade: an agent in panic warns its neighbours, those that reach the threshold panic in turn and warn theirs, and the selling pressure grows faster than the fundamentalists can absorb it. The warnings are forgotten every 30 minutes, so panic only spreads when several neighbours sell at about the same time. Like the fundamentalists, trend followers keep trading during a reservation period in the calibrated model: the transition to Halted has the guard !main.tradeDuringHalt (Section 4.2.4).'
    Body 'In round 8b of the calibration the rate at which a trend follower leaves Neutral was multiplied by an attention level of the market (variable attention of the Main agent), which rose after days with large price moves (Barber & Odean, 2008). The mechanism is kept in the model but switched off (useAttChart = false), because BCP data show no increase in trading after large price moves (Section 3.3).'
    Body 'Three tests confirmed this behaviour. Every trend follower had six neighbours in the network. With the default parameters, the collars were triggered in a normal market, which did not happen with fundamentalists and noise traders only. With 60 trend followers and a herding threshold of 1, the price rose by about 20% in two hours, triggering a 3-minute and a 10-minute reservation (the static collar breached twice in the same direction), and then stayed flat because the agents had run out of cash or shares; this limitation of the initial endowments is addressed in the calibration (Section 6.3).'
}

$content['Calibration'] = {
    Body 'The behavioural parameters of the agents are not observable, so they were calibrated against the stylised facts of the BCP stock fixed in Section 3.2 ({T:facts}). The calibration followed an iterative protocol. In each round the baseline scenario (no news shock, collars as in the real market) was run with 10 replications of 250 trading days, the statistics of {T:facts} were computed by the script compare_model.py for every replication, and the mean over the replications was compared with the BCP values using the acceptance criteria of the table. The median over the replications was also reported, to detect means driven by one or two extreme replications.'
    Body 'Only one mechanism was changed per round, chosen from the diagnosis of the previous round, so that the effect of each change could be identified. Every round is recorded in the calibration log (Appendix D) with the parameter values, the resulting statistics and the decision taken.'
    Body 'The first run of the complete model (round 0, with the parameter values of Appendix E) reproduced only the two volume facts (relation with volatility and persistence, the latter only weakly at 0.04). The market was about five times calmer than BCP (daily volatility of 0.42% against 2.07%), almost no day moved by more than 3%, and daily returns were negatively autocorrelated (-0.14). The diagnosis was that the price was anchored by the fundamentalists to a fundamental value that hardly moved: with one news item every five trading days and a standard deviation of 1% per item, news alone generate a daily volatility of about 0.44%, which matches the model. The mean excess kurtosis of 9.6 came from three replications with reservation periods, while the median was only 0.2.'
    Body 'Round 1 therefore increased the frequency of news from one every five days to about one per day and their size from 1% to 2%, values that, for news alone, give a daily volatility of 2.0%, an excess kurtosis of 2.9 and 13.7% of days with moves above 3%. The model reached four of the seven facts, but the daily volatility rose only to 0.87% and the kurtosis to 75. The reason was that the market faded during the year: the daily volatility fell from 1.22% in the first 50 days to 0.38% after day 150, and 26 of the 35 reservation periods happened in the first 50 days. The fundamentalists and trend followers ran out of cash or shares, so the price stopped following the fundamental value and only caught up in a few large jumps. Round 2 increased their initial endowments tenfold.'
    Body 'In round 2 the mean daily volatility jumped to 3.41%, but this value was driven by a single replication: without it the mean was 2.01%, close to the 2.07% of BCP. In that replication the price fell from 0.84 € to 0.02 € in two days through a sequence of reopening auctions, because the auction of the model had no price limit and each auction price became the new static reference. Euronext limits the reopening auction of PSI constituents to ±6% (assumption 35), so this rule had to be added to the model. The market still faded after about 100 days in most replications, only later than before, and the turbulent first months produced too much volatility clustering (0.45) and negatively autocorrelated returns (-0.16). Round 3 therefore removed the budget constraints of fundamentalists and trend followers, as for the noise traders.'
    Body 'Without budget constraints the market no longer faded: the daily volatility stayed between 3.3% and 3.7% in every 50-day period and the reservation periods were spread over the whole year, and the excess kurtosis (2.39) matched BCP. The market was now too volatile, however (3.60% per day, 30% of days with moves above 3% and about two reservations per day), and daily returns were negatively autocorrelated (-0.34): the price overshot on one day and was corrected on the next. Since news alone generate about 2% of daily volatility, the excess came from the trend followers, so round 4 halved their number from 30 to 15. Round 4 also included the ±6% collar of the reopening auction, which would have capped only 2 of the 5074 auctions of round 3.'
    Body 'Round 4 reproduced six of the seven facts: daily volatility of 2.37%, excess kurtosis of 2.59, volatility clustering of 0.10 and 17% of days with moves above 3%, with about 38 reservation periods per replication spread over the whole year and no auction reaching the ±6% limit. Only the autocorrelation of returns missed its range, by a small margin (-0.105 against a lower bound of -0.1; five of the ten replications were already inside). Since halving the number of trend followers had moved it from -0.34 to -0.105, round 5 reduced their number from 15 to 13.'
    Body 'Round 5 met all seven criteria: daily volatility of 2.24% (BCP: 2.07%), excess kurtosis of 2.22 (2.41), 16.6% of days with moves above 3% (12.9%), a volume-volatility correlation of 0.58 (0.64) and return autocorrelation of -0.07, with about 17 reservation periods per replication spread over the whole year. Three facts are met only narrowly: volatility clustering (0.053 against a threshold of 0.05) and return autocorrelation hold for the mean but only for five of the ten replications each, and volume persistence (0.001) satisfies the criterion of a positive value but is far from the 0.47 of BCP. The model therefore does not reproduce the persistence of trading volume, because most of the daily volume comes from noise traders and the market maker, who trade at a constant rate; this is discussed as a limitation in Chapter 10.'
    Body 'Because volume persistence is one of the two volume facts requested in the feedback on the proposal, round 6 added a daily activity level for the noise traders: their order sizes are multiplied by a factor whose logarithm follows a first-order autoregressive process (persistence 0.7, standard deviation 0.3). Volume persistence rose to 0.66, but the volume-volatility correlation fell from 0.58 to 0.02 and the return autocorrelation to -0.14, because the activity level was unrelated to the news that move the price. Round 7 therefore linked activity to the news: each news event raises the activity level of that day, which then decays over the following days.'
    Body 'Round 7 was run after a correction of the reopening auction (assumption 36), which removed a sequence of 1485 reservation periods observed in one replication of round 6. Both volume facts were now reproduced by the mechanism and not only by the criterion (volume-volatility correlation of 0.25 and volume persistence of 0.60, against 0.64 and 0.47 for BCP), and the return autocorrelation returned to its range (-0.07). Volatility clustering, however, fell to 0.008: news arrive at random, so a volatile day says nothing about the next one. A simplified daily simulation of the news and activity processes showed that letting the arrival rate of news increase with the activity level (news attract news) restores clustering, and round 8 tested this change.'
    Body 'In round 8 the arrival rate of news was multiplied by the activity level (up to three times), with a base rate of 0.0026 per minute and an activity jump of 0.3 per news event. Volatility clustering returned to 0.065, but news then came in waves that were too large: the daily volatility rose to 2.87%, 26% of days moved by more than 3%, the excess kurtosis fell to 0.96 and the volume-volatility correlation to 0.07. Only four facts were met, so this change was discarded.'
    Body 'Round 7 was initially taken as the final model. After the feedback on the realism of the assumptions, round 8b tested two relaxations together with one more attempt to give daily volatility a memory, in a copy of the model and with one change active at a time. Configuration R0 only let fundamentalists and trend followers trade during reservations; configuration A2 added the attention mechanism of the trend followers (Section 4.2.5, attWeight = 0.6); configuration C1 instead made the price dispersion of the noise traders grow with attention (attWeight = 0.3). R0 kept six of the seven facts, which shows that the relaxation does not break the calibration, although the excess kurtosis fell to 1.64 and the return autocorrelation moved close to its lower bound (-0.093). A2 improved every fact with respect to R0: daily volatility of 2.17% (BCP: 2.07%), excess kurtosis of 2.04, return autocorrelation of -0.052, 16.2% of days with moves above 3% and a positive volatility clustering of 0.026. C1 brought clustering to 0.049, just below the threshold, but the return autocorrelation then missed its range (-0.100), and the mechanism is closer to imposing a GARCH process than to a behavioural rule, so it was discarded.'
    Body 'Before the second feedback, the calibration had been closed with configuration A2 of round 8b; its parameter values are listed in Appendix E. It reproduces six of the seven stylised facts, and the two volume facts are reproduced by the mechanism and not only by the criterion (volume-volatility correlation of 0.26 and volume persistence of 0.60, against 0.64 and 0.47 for BCP). Volatility clustering is positive but below the threshold (0.026 against 0.05; BCP: 0.080). This shortfall should be read with the sampling error in mind: with about 250 daily returns per replication, the standard error of a first-order autocorrelation is about 1/√250 ≈ 0.06, larger than the gap to the threshold, so clustering is the noisiest of the seven facts. The robustness check of Section 3.2 points the same way, with clustering ranging from 0.06 to 0.18 across years in the BCP data. Round 5 met all seven criteria, but only because volume persistence (0.001) was positive; the final model was preferred because it reproduces trading volume, which the feedback on the proposal asked to validate together with prices. The model has about 37 reservation periods per year in the baseline scenario. The absence of strong volatility clustering is discussed as a limitation in Chapter 10.'
    Body 'After the second feedback on the realism of the model, the random news were replaced by the real information shocks of 2023–2024 (Section 3.3), the attention mechanism was switched off and the volume parameters and the initial price were set to the values measured in the data. Rounds 0 to 8b remain valid as the construction of the model: they show why it needs large endowments, the auction collar and a persistent activity level. From round 9 onwards the model replays the 509 trading days of 2023–2024, so that its output is compared with BCP over the same days and events; each round uses 10 replications.'
    Body 'Round 9 ran the re-specified model without any further change. Volatility clustering was reproduced for the first time (0.094 against 0.080 for BCP), because the real sector shocks come in clusters, and the volume-volatility correlation rose to 0.55. The model also followed BCP day by day (the correlation between the mean daily return of the replications and the real return was 0.74). However, the market was calmer than BCP (daily volatility of 1.63% against 2.07%) and the excess kurtosis rose to 5.56: the identified shocks explain only about 58% of the variance of BCP, and without the remaining diffuse movements the large real jumps dominate the tails.'
    Body 'To find whether the unobservable parameters of the agents could generate the missing part, two designed experiments were run on the round 9 model, each parameter at -50% and +50% of its value ({T:paramEffects}). A fractional factorial design 2^(6-1) of resolution VI (32 combinations x 5 replications) covered the numbers of trend followers, fundamentalists and noise traders, the price dispersion of the noise traders and the thresholds of fundamentalists and trend followers; an analysis of variance shows that the number of trend followers, the threshold of the trend followers and the number of noise traders explain most of the variation of every stylised fact, while the price dispersion of the noise traders and the band of the fundamentalists hardly matter. A Plackett-Burman screening design (12 combinations x 5 replications, with a dummy column as a noise benchmark) covered ten other unobservable parameters and showed that the lifetime of orders, the order rate of the noise traders, the decision rates of trend followers and fundamentalists and the duration of panic matter as much as the main parameters of the ANOVA, while the parameters of the market maker hardly matter. In both experiments, every change that raises volatility and lowers kurtosis also pushes the autocorrelation of returns towards -0.2: the agents generate volatility through overshooting and reversals. None of the 44 combinations met the seven criteria.'
    Body 'Since the missing movements of BCP behave like new information and not like reversals, they were added as the unidentified information channel described in Section 3.3, and the volume parameters were re-estimated jointly with it. Round 10, with independent daily draws, matched volatility (2.10%), kurtosis (2.25), return autocorrelation (0.001), the share of days above 3% (13.1%) and both volume facts, but volatility clustering fell to 0.043, because independent draws remove the short memory of these movements. Round 11 drew them in blocks of 8 consecutive days and reproduced all seven stylised facts ({T:round11}). The parameters of the agents were not changed between rounds 9 and 11: everything that changed came from data.'
    Tbl 'Final calibrated model (round 11) against BCP, 2023–2024' @('Stylised fact', 'BCP', 'Model (mean of 10 replications)', 'Criterion met') @(
        @('Daily volatility', '0.0207', '0.0212', 'Yes'),
        @('Excess kurtosis', '2.41', '2.08', 'Yes'),
        @('Volatility clustering (autocorr. |r|)', '0.080', '0.077', 'Yes'),
        @('Autocorrelation of returns', '0.004', '-0.017', 'Yes'),
        @('Days with |r| > 3%', '12.9%', '14.1%', 'Yes'),
        @('Correlation volume-|r|', '0.636', '0.546', 'Yes'),
        @('Autocorrelation of volume', '0.470', '0.462', 'Yes')
    ) @(6.0, 2.2, 4.4, 2.6) 'round11'
    Tbl 'Main effects of the unobservable parameters (change from -50% to +50%)' @('Parameter', 'Experiment', 'Daily volatility (pp)', 'Excess kurtosis', 'Autocorr. of returns') @(
        @('nChartists (number of trend followers)', 'ANOVA', '+1.06', '~0', '-0.22'),
        @('trendThreshold (trend follower threshold)', 'ANOVA', '-0.81', '+3.9', '+0.19'),
        @('nNoise (number of noise traders)', 'ANOVA', '-0.77', '+3.9', '+0.23'),
        @('nFundamentalists', 'ANOVA', '-0.44', 'small', 'small'),
        @('noiseSigma, fundBand', 'ANOVA', 'small', 'noiseSigma -1.7', 'small'),
        @('orderLife (lifetime of orders)', 'Screening', '-0.99', '+4.9', '+0.22'),
        @('noiseRate (orders per minute of noise traders)', 'Screening', '-0.61', '+4.2', '+0.25'),
        @('chartRate (decisions per minute of trend followers)', 'Screening', '+0.59', '-3.6', '-0.15'),
        @('fundRate (decisions per minute of fundamentalists)', 'Screening', '-0.74', '-1.1', '+0.05'),
        @('panicDuration', 'Screening', '-0.70', '~0', '+0.16'),
        @('mmSpread, mmQty (market maker)', 'Screening', '~0', '~0', '~0')
    ) @(5.6, 2.0, 2.6, 2.4, 2.6) 'paramEffects'
    Body 'The acceptance criteria of {T:facts} were set at the start of the project by judgement, informed by the year-to-year variation of the facts of BCP and by the qualitative tests used in the agent-based literature, and some of them are loose (for example, any positive correlation between volume and volatility). They were therefore complemented by a criterion derived from the data: the 95% interval of each fact that BCP itself could have produced by chance over a period of the same length, obtained by resampling the real trading days in blocks of 10 consecutive days (3000 block-bootstrap samples). A model value inside this interval cannot be distinguished statistically from BCP. The calibrated model meets this stricter criterion for all seven facts ({T:bootTrain}), although the volume-volatility correlation is close to the lower limit.'
    Tbl 'Data-based acceptance intervals (block bootstrap, 95%) and the calibrated model, 2023–2024' @('Stylised fact', 'BCP', '95% interval of BCP', 'Initial criterion', 'Round 11') @(
        @('Daily volatility', '0.0207', '0.0184 to 0.0231', '0.0155 to 0.0259', '0.0212'),
        @('Excess kurtosis', '2.41', '0.91 to 3.77', '1.21 to 3.62', '2.08'),
        @('Volatility clustering', '0.080', '-0.032 to 0.151', '> 0.05', '0.077'),
        @('Autocorrelation of returns', '0.004', '-0.083 to 0.071', '-0.1 to 0.1', '-0.017'),
        @('Days with |r| > 3%', '12.9%', '9.6% to 16.5%', '6.5% to 19.4%', '14.1%'),
        @('Correlation volume-|r|', '0.636', '0.540 to 0.705', '> 0', '0.546'),
        @('Autocorrelation of volume', '0.470', '0.275 to 0.543', '> 0', '0.462')
    ) @(4.4, 1.6, 3.4, 3.2, 1.8) 'bootTrain'
    Body 'The calibration was closed with round 11. Volatility clustering, which had failed since round 7, is reproduced, and the remaining differences to BCP are small. With about 42% of its variance coming from unidentified movements, the model does not explain why those movements occur; it reproduces their statistical properties. The influential unobservable parameters identified above are used in the sensitivity analysis of the crash scenarios (Chapter 7), to check whether the recommendation on the collars depends on them.'
}

$content['Validation'] = {
    Body 'The calibrated model was validated out of sample on January 2025 to October 2026 (460 trading days), a period that was not used in the calibration and that includes the market-wide sell-off of April 2025. The model was run with the real shocks of this period and no change to any parameter. To keep the test honest, the betas to the sector and to the Portuguese market and the reaction day of each type of announcement were those estimated on 2023–2024, the unidentified movements were still drawn from those of 2023–2024, and only the real 2025–2026 returns of the sector and of the Portuguese market and the dated events (118 BCP announcements, 8 political events and 7 changes in the sovereign rating) were used. The test was run once, after the calibration was closed. The same rules for selecting events cover this period at least as well as the calibration period: 25% of the days with moves above 3% have no identified source, against 46% in 2023–2024.'
    Tbl 'Out-of-sample test: model against BCP, January 2025 to October 2026' @('Stylised fact', 'BCP 2025–2026', '95% interval of BCP', 'Model (mean of 10 replications)', 'Initial criterion met') @(
        @('Daily volatility', '0.0184', '0.0160 to 0.0207', '0.0207', 'Yes'),
        @('Excess kurtosis', '2.20', '0.30 to 4.56', '2.13', 'Yes'),
        @('Volatility clustering (autocorr. |r|)', '0.156', '0.006 to 0.240', '0.097', 'Yes'),
        @('Autocorrelation of returns', '-0.051', '-0.172 to 0.088', '0.033', 'Yes'),
        @('Days with |r| > 3%', '11.3%', '7.4% to 15.4%', '13.8%', 'Yes'),
        @('Correlation volume-|r|', '0.535', '0.438 to 0.673', '0.534', 'Yes'),
        @('Autocorrelation of volume', '0.251', '0.077 to 0.427', '0.464', 'Yes')
    ) @(4.6, 2.2, 3.2, 3.2, 2.2) 'oosTest'
    Body 'The model meets the initial criteria for the seven stylised facts of BCP in the test period and the stricter data-based intervals for five of them (volatility is at the upper limit and volume persistence is above it), and the mean daily return of the replications has a correlation of 0.84 with the real daily return. This correlation is not a forecast by the model: it follows from the model receiving the real information of each day, and it shows only that the agents transmit that information to prices. Three differences should be noted. Volatility is 12% higher than in BCP, because the model draws the unidentified movements of 2023–2024, which were larger than those of 2025–2026, where the market explained a larger share of the variance (74% against 39%). Volatility clustering is lower than in BCP (0.097 against 0.156), as the agitation after the April 2025 sell-off was more persistent than the model generates. Volume persistence is higher than in BCP (0.46 against 0.25), since the volume parameters were measured in 2023–2024, when the real persistence was 0.47.'
    Body 'A second out-of-sample test covered January 2020 to December 2021 (509 trading days), which includes the COVID-19 crash of March 2020, a crisis period for BCP (daily volatility of 2.77%, against 2.07% in the calibration period). The test followed the same rules and was also run once, with no change to any parameter: the betas, reaction days and unidentified movements were those of 2023–2024, the initial price was the real close of 31 December 2019 (0.2028 EUR), and the dated events were the 87 BCP announcements of 2020–2021, 7 political events (State Budgets, the presidential election, the rejection of the 2022 Budget and the dissolution of Parliament) and 2 changes in the sovereign rating. The declaration of the state of emergency (18 March 2020) was deliberately not added, because it is not in any of the categories of events fixed before the tests; the crash reaches the model through the real fall of the European banking sector. In this period the sector and the Portuguese market explain 86% of the variance of BCP, and half of the days with moves above 3% are explained by them ({T:oosCovid}).'
    Tbl 'Out-of-sample test in a crisis: model against BCP, January 2020 to December 2021' @('Stylised fact', 'BCP 2020–2021', '95% interval of BCP', 'Model (mean of 10 replications)', 'Initial criterion met') @(
        @('Daily volatility', '0.0277', '0.0237 to 0.0324', '0.0305', 'Yes'),
        @('Excess kurtosis', '5.86', '1.00 to 8.82', '5.08', 'Yes'),
        @('Volatility clustering (autocorr. |r|)', '0.132', '0.034 to 0.178', '0.105', 'Yes'),
        @('Autocorrelation of returns', '0.081', '-0.016 to 0.151', '-0.001', 'Yes'),
        @('Days with |r| > 3%', '19.8%', '15.8% to 24.9%', '23.8%', 'Yes'),
        @('Correlation volume-|r|', '0.570', '0.509 to 0.628', '0.657', 'Yes'),
        @('Autocorrelation of volume', '0.686', '0.531 to 0.676', '0.508', 'Yes')
    ) @(4.6, 2.2, 3.2, 3.2, 2.2) 'oosCovid'
    Body 'The model calibrated in a calm period reproduces the crisis: it meets the initial criteria for the seven facts and the data-based intervals for the five price facts, including the high volatility, the heavy tails (excess kurtosis of 5.1 against 5.9) and the share of days with moves above 3% (24% against 20%). Volatility is about 10% above that of BCP. In this period the identified sources and the unidentified movements partly offset each other (with the betas of 2023–2024 their separate shares of the variance of BCP add up to about 127%), whereas the model adds them as independent sources, so it slightly overstates the variance. The two volume facts are outside the intervals: volume is more tied to the size of the daily shock than in BCP (0.657 against 0.570) and less persistent (0.508 against 0.686). The interval for volume persistence should be read with care, because the volume of BCP changed level strongly during 2020–2021 and the block bootstrap cuts this long memory, so that the real value itself is slightly above its interval. The correlation between the mean daily return of the replications and the real return is 0.81, for the same reason as in the previous test.'
    Tbl 'Summary of the out-of-sample tests (parameters fixed in 2023–2024)' @('Test period', 'Market conditions', 'Initial criteria', '95% intervals of BCP', 'Facts outside the intervals') @(
        @('2025–2026 (460 days)', 'Calm, with the April 2025 sell-off', '7 / 7', '5 / 7', 'Volatility (at the upper limit), volume persistence (above)'),
        @('2020–2021 (509 days)', 'Crisis (COVID-19 crash)', '7 / 7', '5 / 7', 'Correlation volume-|r| (above), volume persistence (below)')
    ) @(3.2, 3.6, 2.2, 2.6, 5.4) 'oosSummary'
    Body 'Both tests lead to the same conclusion ({T:oosSummary}): with its parameters fixed, the model reproduces the price facts of BCP in a calm period and in a crisis, which supports its use for the crash scenarios of Chapter 7. Volume is the weakest part of the model out of sample, so the liquidity indicators that depend on traded volume should be compared between scenarios rather than read in absolute terms.'
}

